Object.assign(window.IND_FEED_BODY, {"st-fk.satisar":{"route":"#/story/fk.satisar","art":"art/story/fk-satisar.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.satisar:scenes:0:text","title":"The Valley That Was a Lake","body":"Long, long ago — so says Kashmir's own oldest telling — there was no valley of Kashmir at all. Between the ring of great mountains lay one enormous lake, vast and deep and cold, called Satisar, the lake of the goddess Sati.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sh-fk.satisar":{"route":"#/story/fk.satisar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.satisar:hook","title":"The Valley That Was a Lake","body":"Kashmir's own oldest story says the whole valley was once one enormous lake — and that it took a sage with great patience to let the land out.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sm-fk.satisar":{"route":"#/story/fk.satisar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.satisar:scenes:4:text","title":"The Valley That Was a Lake — the middle","body":"And as the water sank, the valley rose out of it like a gift being unwrapped: ridges first, then slopes, then the wide green floor with its rivers already running — the Jhelum winding down the middle like a signature.","why":"A moment from The Valley That Was a Lake"},"sl-fk.satisar":{"route":"#/story/fk.satisar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.satisar:moral","title":"What The Valley That Was a Lake says","body":"Under every settled, beautiful place there is an older story of how it was made livable — and someone patient in it.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.satisar-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.satisar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.satisar:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Valley That Was a Lake","text":"केसर","roman":"kesar","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["saffron","market","thumb"],"a":0,"after":"केसर (kesar) means “saffron”."},"why":"A word from The Valley That Was a Lake"},"sp-fk.satisar":{"route":"#/story/fk.satisar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.satisar:place:0","title":"The Valley That Was a Lake","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jammu & Kashmir","Manipur","Uttarakhand"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jammu & Kashmir."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.himal-nagrai":{"route":"#/story/fk.himal-nagrai","art":"art/story/fk-himal-nagrai.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.himal-nagrai:scenes:0:text","title":"Himal and the Serpent Prince","body":"Kashmir is a valley of springs — cold, clear pools that rise out of the earth under the chinar trees. And Kashmiris have always said that springs are doors: that the naga folk, the serpent people of the old stories, live in shining kingdoms on the other side of the water.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sh-fk.himal-nagrai":{"route":"#/story/fk.himal-nagrai","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.himal-nagrai:hook","title":"Himal and the Serpent Prince","body":"Kashmir's most beloved fairy tale begins with a spring that was not just a spring, and a prince who was not just a prince.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sm-fk.himal-nagrai":{"route":"#/story/fk.himal-nagrai","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.himal-nagrai:scenes:4:text","title":"Himal and the Serpent Prince — the middle","body":"But a princess of the land and a prince of the water — both their worlds had opinions about that. His people wanted him below. Hers could not understand what he was.","why":"A moment from Himal and the Serpent Prince"},"sl-fk.himal-nagrai":{"route":"#/story/fk.himal-nagrai","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.himal-nagrai:moral","title":"What Himal and the Serpent Prince says","body":"Love does not check which world someone comes from. It is everyone else who checks.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.himal-nagrai-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.himal-nagrai","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.himal-nagrai:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from Himal and the Serpent Prince","text":"झरना","roman":"jharna","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["spring, stream","load","thirst"],"a":0,"after":"झरना (jharna) means “spring, stream”."},"why":"A word from Himal and the Serpent Prince"},"sp-fk.himal-nagrai":{"route":"#/story/fk.himal-nagrai","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.himal-nagrai:place:0","title":"Himal and the Serpent Prince","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jammu & Kashmir","Rajasthan","Bihar"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jammu & Kashmir."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.lal-ded":{"route":"#/story/fk.lal-ded","art":"art/story/fk-lal-ded.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.lal-ded:scenes:0:text","title":"The Woman Everyone Claims","body":"Around seven hundred years ago, in the Kashmir valley, there lived a woman called Lalla. She had been married young into a house that was unkind to her, and one day — the tellings agree on this — she walked out of that life entirely, to seek God.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sh-fk.lal-ded":{"route":"#/story/fk.lal-ded","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.lal-ded:hook","title":"The Woman Everyone Claims","body":"Seven hundred years ago a woman walked the Kashmir valley speaking short, blazing verses. Kashmiris are still quoting her — and still lovingly arguing about whose she is.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sm-fk.lal-ded":{"route":"#/story/fk.lal-ded","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.lal-ded:scenes:4:text","title":"The Woman Everyone Claims — the middle","body":"And then there is the beautiful argument. Kashmiri Hindus revere her as Lalleshwari, a great saint of their tradition. Kashmiri Muslims revere her as Lalla Arifa, a great mystic of theirs. Both keep her stories. Both quote her verses. Whose is she?","why":"A moment from The Woman Everyone Claims"},"sl-fk.lal-ded":{"route":"#/story/fk.lal-ded","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.lal-ded:moral","title":"What The Woman Everyone Claims says","body":"Speak truth plainly enough and everyone will claim you — because truth, spoken plainly, belongs to everyone.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.lal-ded-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.lal-ded","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.lal-ded:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Woman Everyone Claims","text":"कविता","roman":"kavita","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["poem","morning","to tell"],"a":0,"after":"कविता (kavita) means “poem”."},"why":"A word from The Woman Everyone Claims"},"sp-fk.lal-ded":{"route":"#/story/fk.lal-ded","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.lal-ded:place:0","title":"The Woman Everyone Claims","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jammu & Kashmir","West Bengal","Gujarat"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jammu & Kashmir."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.dal-morning":{"route":"#/story/fk.dal-morning","art":"art/story/fk-dal-morning.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dal-morning:scenes:0:text","title":"The Lake That Is a Neighbourhood","body":"Before sunrise on Dal Lake, in Srinagar, the water is a sheet of grey silk with mountains standing round it — and out of the dark, from every direction, come boats. Long, slim wooden boats called shikaras, paddled with heart-shaped oars, loaded to the gunwales with vegetables.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sh-fk.dal-morning":{"route":"#/story/fk.dal-morning","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dal-morning:hook","title":"The Lake That Is a Neighbourhood","body":"On Dal Lake in Srinagar, houses float, gardens float, the school run goes by boat — and the vegetable market opens on open water before the sun is properly up.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sm-fk.dal-morning":{"route":"#/story/fk.dal-morning","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dal-morning:scenes:4:text","title":"The Lake That Is a Neighbourhood — the middle","body":"The gardens float too. Farmers weave beds of reeds and lake-weed, spread them with rich lake mud, and grow vegetables on rafts — gardens you can tow. A grandfather explained it to his visiting grandson, who did not believe a word until he stood on one.","why":"A moment from The Lake That Is a Neighbourhood"},"sl-fk.dal-morning":{"route":"#/story/fk.dal-morning","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dal-morning:moral","title":"What The Lake That Is a Neighbourhood says","body":"People make a home wherever they are — even on water. Given time, they make it beautiful too.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.dal-morning-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.dal-morning","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dal-morning:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Lake That Is a Neighbourhood","text":"सब्ज़ी","roman":"sabzi","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["vegetable","nest","truth"],"a":0,"after":"सब्ज़ी (sabzi) means “vegetable”."},"why":"A word from The Lake That Is a Neighbourhood"},"sp-fk.dal-morning":{"route":"#/story/fk.dal-morning","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dal-morning:place:0","title":"The Lake That Is a Neighbourhood","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jammu & Kashmir","Chhattisgarh","Lakshadweep"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jammu & Kashmir."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.boonyi-year":{"route":"#/story/fk.boonyi-year","art":"art/story/fk-boonyi-year.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.boonyi-year:scenes:0:text","title":"A Year Under the Chinar","body":"In Kashmiri it is called the boon; in books, the chinar. It is a plane tree, and in Kashmir it grows to a size that has to be seen to be believed — trunks wider than a room, crowns like green weather, some trees so old that Mughal emperors may have sat under them as saplings.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sh-fk.boonyi-year":{"route":"#/story/fk.boonyi-year","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.boonyi-year:hook","title":"A Year Under the Chinar","body":"Kashmir measures its year by one enormous tree — green, greener, on fire, and bare — and has done for five hundred years.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sm-fk.boonyi-year":{"route":"#/story/fk.boonyi-year","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.boonyi-year:scenes:4:text","title":"A Year Under the Chinar — the middle","body":"Children gather the fallen leaves — five-pointed, big as a spread hand — and everyone with a book has pressed one flat between the pages. The dry leaves have older uses too.","why":"A moment from A Year Under the Chinar"},"sl-fk.boonyi-year":{"route":"#/story/fk.boonyi-year","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.boonyi-year:moral","title":"What A Year Under the Chinar says","body":"Plant patience in the middle of the village, and for five hundred years everything good happens in its shade.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.boonyi-year-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.boonyi-year","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.boonyi-year:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from A Year Under the Chinar","text":"पतझड़","roman":"patjhad","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["autumn","net","tunnel"],"a":0,"after":"पतझड़ (patjhad) means “autumn”."},"why":"A word from A Year Under the Chinar"},"sw-fk.boonyi-year-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.boonyi-year","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.boonyi-year:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from A Year Under the Chinar","text":"मौसम","roman":"mausam","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["season","nest","tunnel"],"a":0,"after":"मौसम (mausam) means “season”."},"why":"A word from A Year Under the Chinar"},"sp-fk.boonyi-year":{"route":"#/story/fk.boonyi-year","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.boonyi-year:place:0","title":"A Year Under the Chinar","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jammu & Kashmir","Haryana","Odisha"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jammu & Kashmir."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.kangri-winter":{"route":"#/story/fk.kangri-winter","art":"art/story/fk-kangri-winter.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kangri-winter:scenes:0:text","title":"The Fire You Can Carry","body":"Kashmiri winter is serious winter — snow to the windowsills, icicles like fence posts, breath like smoke indoors. Its hardest stretch even has a name: Chillai Kalan, \"the great cold,\" forty days in the deep of winter when the valley truly freezes.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sh-fk.kangri-winter":{"route":"#/story/fk.kangri-winter","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kangri-winter:hook","title":"The Fire You Can Carry","body":"The coldest stretch of the Kashmiri winter has a name, like a visiting relative. Kashmiris meet it with a woollen gown — and a personal fire, carried like a secret.","why":"From The Valley and the Lake"},"sm-fk.kangri-winter":{"route":"#/story/fk.kangri-winter","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kangri-winter:scenes:4:text","title":"The Fire You Can Carry — the middle","body":"A child from outside, visiting winter relatives, once watched everyone sitting comfortably in a freezing room, mysteriously content, and demanded to know the trick.","why":"A moment from The Fire You Can Carry"},"sl-fk.kangri-winter":{"route":"#/story/fk.kangri-winter","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kangri-winter:moral","title":"What The Fire You Can Carry says","body":"You cannot argue with winter, but you can out-think it — with wool, willow, and a small fire carried close.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.kangri-winter-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.kangri-winter","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kangri-winter:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Fire You Can Carry","text":"अंगारा","roman":"angaara","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["ember","paddy","wallet"],"a":0,"after":"अंगारा (angaara) means “ember”."},"why":"A word from The Fire You Can Carry"},"sw-fk.kangri-winter-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.kangri-winter","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kangri-winter:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Fire You Can Carry","text":"ऊन","roman":"oon","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["wool","music","tortoise"],"a":0,"after":"ऊन (oon) means “wool”."},"why":"A word from The Fire You Can Carry"},"sp-fk.kangri-winter":{"route":"#/story/fk.kangri-winter","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kangri-winter:place:0","title":"The Fire You Can Carry","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jammu & Kashmir","Meghalaya","Tripura"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jammu & Kashmir."},"why":"A map question"},"st-it.rakhigarhi":{"route":"#/story/it.rakhigarhi","art":"art/story/it-rakhigarhi.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.rakhigarhi:scenes:0:text","title":"The Village on Top of a Lost City","body":"In Hisar district of Haryana there is a farming village called Rakhigarhi — buffaloes, wheat, brick houses, the usual weather. And beside and beneath it, a set of great dusty mounds, where children have always flown kites and grazed goats.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-it.rakhigarhi":{"route":"#/story/it.rakhigarhi","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.rakhigarhi:hook","title":"The Village on Top of a Lost City","body":"The children of one Haryana village always played on the big dusty mounds outside it. Nobody knew the mounds were the roofs of a city five thousand years old.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-it.rakhigarhi":{"route":"#/story/it.rakhigarhi","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.rakhigarhi:scenes:4:text","title":"The Village on Top of a Lost City — the middle","body":"Out of the earth came the everyday life of people from fifty centuries ago: mud-brick houses along straight lanes, kitchen hearths, storage jars, toy carts with little clay wheels, bangles, and thousands of those beads — for Rakhigarhi had been a great bead-making town, its workshops famous, it seems, far and wide.","why":"A moment from The Village on Top of a Lost City"},"sl-it.rakhigarhi":{"route":"#/story/it.rakhigarhi","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.rakhigarhi:moral","title":"What The Village on Top of a Lost City says","body":"The ground under ordinary life is often extraordinary. The difference is somebody deciding to look carefully.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-it.rakhigarhi-0":{"route":"#/story/it.rakhigarhi","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.rakhigarhi:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Village on Top of a Lost City","text":"टीला","roman":"teela","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["mound","net","tunnel"],"a":0,"after":"टीला (teela) means “mound”."},"why":"A word from The Village on Top of a Lost City"},"sw-it.rakhigarhi-1":{"route":"#/story/it.rakhigarhi","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.rakhigarhi:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Village on Top of a Lost City","text":"मिट्टी","roman":"mitti","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["earth, clay","patience","wealth"],"a":0,"after":"मिट्टी (mitti) means “earth, clay”."},"why":"A word from The Village on Top of a Lost City"},"sw-it.rakhigarhi-2":{"route":"#/story/it.rakhigarhi","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.rakhigarhi:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Village on Top of a Lost City","text":"खुदाई","roman":"khudai","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["digging","patience","wealth"],"a":0,"after":"खुदाई (khudai) means “digging”."},"why":"A word from The Village on Top of a Lost City"},"sp-it.rakhigarhi":{"route":"#/story/it.rakhigarhi","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.rakhigarhi:place:0","title":"The Village on Top of a Lost City","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Haryana","Puducherry","Assam"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Haryana."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.akhara-pond":{"route":"#/story/fk.akhara-pond","art":"art/story/fk-akhara-pond.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.akhara-pond:scenes:0:text","title":"The Wrestlers by the Village Pond","body":"Every proper Haryana village has its johad — the village pond — where the buffaloes soak like glossy black islands and the herons do their slow patrolling. And near the pond, in village after village, there is something else: the akhara. The wrestling pit.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.akhara-pond":{"route":"#/story/fk.akhara-pond","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.akhara-pond:hook","title":"The Wrestlers by the Village Pond","body":"In a Haryana village, the day starts before the sun — at the pond, where the buffaloes soak, and beside it, where the wrestlers dig their pit.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.akhara-pond":{"route":"#/story/fk.akhara-pond","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.akhara-pond:scenes:4:text","title":"The Wrestlers by the Village Pond — the middle","body":"For a long, long time, the pit was only for boys. Then, not so many years ago, girls in Haryana began stepping into the akharas too — and some fathers and coaches backed them against every frown in the district.","why":"A moment from The Wrestlers by the Village Pond"},"sl-fk.akhara-pond":{"route":"#/story/fk.akhara-pond","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.akhara-pond:moral","title":"What The Wrestlers by the Village Pond says","body":"Strength is not a gift some people are given. It is dawn after dawn after dawn, stacked up quietly.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.akhara-pond-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.akhara-pond","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.akhara-pond:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Wrestlers by the Village Pond","text":"कुश्ती","roman":"kushti","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["wrestling","path","way"],"a":0,"after":"कुश्ती (kushti) means “wrestling”."},"why":"A word from The Wrestlers by the Village Pond"},"sw-fk.akhara-pond-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.akhara-pond","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.akhara-pond:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Wrestlers by the Village Pond","text":"दंगल","roman":"dangal","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["wrestling bout, tournament","path","way"],"a":0,"after":"दंगल (dangal) means “wrestling bout, tournament”."},"why":"A word from The Wrestlers by the Village Pond"},"sp-fk.akhara-pond":{"route":"#/story/fk.akhara-pond","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.akhara-pond:place:0","title":"The Wrestlers by the Village Pond","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Haryana","Uttar Pradesh","Goa"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Haryana."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.surajkund":{"route":"#/story/fk.surajkund","art":"art/story/fk-surajkund.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.surajkund:scenes:0:text","title":"The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders","body":"In Faridabad, in southern Haryana, on the rocky old shoulders of the Aravalli hills, there is an ancient stone pool built in a great curve of steps — shaped, people have always said, like the rising sun. Its name says the same: Surajkund. The pool of the sun.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.surajkund":{"route":"#/story/fk.surajkund","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.surajkund:hook","title":"The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders","body":"A thousand-year-old pool shaped like the rising sun stands quiet most of the year. Then, every spring, all of India's craftspeople arrive at once.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.surajkund":{"route":"#/story/fk.surajkund","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.surajkund:scenes:4:text","title":"The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders — the middle","body":"A girl at the mela with her parents watched a potter throw a pot in under a minute and asked him how long it took to learn to do that.","why":"A moment from The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders"},"sl-fk.surajkund":{"route":"#/story/fk.surajkund","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.surajkund:moral","title":"What The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders says","body":"A crafted thing is time made visible — a minute of skill standing on a hundred years of learning.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.surajkund-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.surajkund","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.surajkund:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders","text":"कुंड","roman":"kund","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["pool, tank","path","wealth"],"a":0,"after":"कुंड (kund) means “pool, tank”."},"why":"A word from The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders"},"sw-fk.surajkund-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.surajkund","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.surajkund:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders","text":"कारीगर","roman":"kaarigar","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["craftsperson","punch","work"],"a":0,"after":"कारीगर (kaarigar) means “craftsperson”."},"why":"A word from The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders"},"sp-fk.surajkund":{"route":"#/story/fk.surajkund","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.surajkund:place:0","title":"The Sun Pool Where India Comes to Sell Wonders","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Haryana","Chandigarh","Ladakh"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Haryana."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.kurukshetra-waters":{"route":"#/story/fk.kurukshetra-waters","art":"art/story/fk-kurukshetra-waters.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kurukshetra-waters:scenes:0:text","title":"The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken","body":"Drive north through Haryana's wheat and mustard and you come to a name that makes the whole Mahabharata sit up: Kurukshetra. This ordinary-looking, extraordinary stretch of land is where tradition places the epic's great turning — and much more besides.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.kurukshetra-waters":{"route":"#/story/fk.kurukshetra-waters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kurukshetra-waters:hook","title":"The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken","body":"Haryana's flat green farmland holds the stage of India's greatest epic — and a banyan tree under which, tradition says, the most famous conversation in the world took place.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.kurukshetra-waters":{"route":"#/story/fk.kurukshetra-waters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kurukshetra-waters:scenes:4:text","title":"The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken — the middle","body":"And then there is the quietest, mightiest spot of all: Jyotisar, on the old pool's edge, where a banyan tree stands. Tradition holds that under this banyan's ancestor — some say this very tree — Krishna spoke the Bhagavad Gita to Arjuna.","why":"A moment from The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken"},"sl-fk.kurukshetra-waters":{"route":"#/story/fk.kurukshetra-waters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kurukshetra-waters:moral","title":"What The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken says","body":"When someone loses heart, the greatest help is not a push — it is somebody willing to stop and talk it all the way through.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.kurukshetra-waters-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.kurukshetra-waters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kurukshetra-waters:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken","text":"तीर्थ","roman":"teerth","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["pilgrimage place","plant","white"],"a":0,"after":"तीर्थ (teerth) means “pilgrimage place”."},"why":"A word from The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken"},"sw-fk.kurukshetra-waters-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.kurukshetra-waters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kurukshetra-waters:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken","text":"बरगद","roman":"bargad","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["banyan","plant","white"],"a":0,"after":"बरगद (bargad) means “banyan”."},"why":"A word from The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken"},"sw-fk.kurukshetra-waters-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.kurukshetra-waters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kurukshetra-waters:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken","text":"जल","roman":"jal","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["water","path","wealth"],"a":0,"after":"जल (jal) means “water”."},"why":"A word from The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken"},"sp-fk.kurukshetra-waters":{"route":"#/story/fk.kurukshetra-waters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.kurukshetra-waters:place:0","title":"The Land Where the Gita Was Spoken","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Haryana","Himachal Pradesh","Nagaland"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Haryana."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.bail-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.bail-race","art":"art/story/fk-bail-race.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bail-race:scenes:0:text","title":"The Race of the Two Bullock Carts","body":"They tell this one in the villages of Haryana, where a good pair of bullocks was once a family's tractor, truck and pride all in one, and where a bullock-cart race at fair time could keep the district in arguments for a year.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.bail-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.bail-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bail-race:hook","title":"The Race of the Two Bullock Carts","body":"One farmer had the shiniest new bullocks in the village. One had two old steady ones. Naturally, there had to be a race.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.bail-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.bail-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bail-race:scenes:4:text","title":"The Race of the Two Bullock Carts — the middle","body":"At the drop of the pagri they were off — and Ramphal's white pair went like the wind. By the pond they were ten lengths ahead. Ramphal stood up in the cart waving to the crowd, which is always, in any story, the moment to start worrying.","why":"A moment from The Race of the Two Bullock Carts"},"sl-fk.bail-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.bail-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bail-race:moral","title":"What The Race of the Two Bullock Carts says","body":"Speed wins the straight parts. Steadiness wins the corners — and every course has corners.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.bail-race-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.bail-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bail-race:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Race of the Two Bullock Carts","text":"बैल","roman":"bail","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["ox, bullock","patience","wealth"],"a":0,"after":"बैल (bail) means “ox, bullock”."},"why":"A word from The Race of the Two Bullock Carts"},"sw-fk.bail-race-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.bail-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bail-race:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Race of the Two Bullock Carts","text":"धीरे","roman":"dheere","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["slowly","protection","work"],"a":0,"after":"धीरे (dheere) means “slowly”."},"why":"A word from The Race of the Two Bullock Carts"},"sp-fk.bail-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.bail-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bail-race:place:0","title":"The Race of the Two Bullock Carts","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Haryana","Maharashtra","Tamil Nadu"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Haryana."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.charpai-darbar":{"route":"#/story/fk.charpai-darbar","art":"art/story/fk-charpai-darbar.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.charpai-darbar:scenes:0:text","title":"The Parliament Under the Banyan","body":"In the evening, when the buffaloes have been milked and the tools hung up, the charpais come out — the wooden-framed rope cots of the north — and are set down in the open, under the big banyan or beside somebody's gate, in a rough friendly circle.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.charpai-darbar":{"route":"#/story/fk.charpai-darbar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.charpai-darbar:hook","title":"The Parliament Under the Banyan","body":"Every Haryana village has an open-air parliament. Its benches are rope cots, its roof is a banyan tree, and its sessions begin when the day's work ends.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.charpai-darbar":{"route":"#/story/fk.charpai-darbar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.charpai-darbar:scenes:4:text","title":"The Parliament Under the Banyan — the middle","body":"And quietly, between the jokes, the circle does real work. A quarrel over a field boundary comes up — sideways, as these things do. Everyone has a say. Somebody remembers how the grandfathers settled it.","why":"A moment from The Parliament Under the Banyan"},"sl-fk.charpai-darbar":{"route":"#/story/fk.charpai-darbar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.charpai-darbar:moral","title":"What The Parliament Under the Banyan says","body":"A village stays a village because its people sit down together in the evening with nothing but talk — and the talk turns out to be everything.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.charpai-darbar-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.charpai-darbar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.charpai-darbar:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Parliament Under the Banyan","text":"चारपाई","roman":"charpai","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["rope cot","quietly","zero"],"a":0,"after":"चारपाई (charpai) means “rope cot”."},"why":"A word from The Parliament Under the Banyan"},"sw-fk.charpai-darbar-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.charpai-darbar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.charpai-darbar:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Parliament Under the Banyan","text":"बातें","roman":"baatein","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["talk, conversation","quietly","zero"],"a":0,"after":"बातें (baatein) means “talk, conversation”."},"why":"A word from The Parliament Under the Banyan"},"sw-fk.charpai-darbar-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.charpai-darbar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.charpai-darbar:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Parliament Under the Banyan","text":"हुक्का","roman":"hukka","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["hookah pipe","river","a flute"],"a":0,"after":"हुक्का (hukka) means “hookah pipe”."},"why":"A word from The Parliament Under the Banyan"},"sp-fk.charpai-darbar":{"route":"#/story/fk.charpai-darbar","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.charpai-darbar:place:0","title":"The Parliament Under the Banyan","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Haryana","Punjab","Arunachal Pradesh"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Haryana."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.dulla-bhatti":{"route":"#/story/fk.dulla-bhatti","art":"art/story/fk-dulla-bhatti.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dulla-bhatti:scenes:0:text","title":"The Song Around the Lohri Fire","body":"On Lohri night, in the middle of the Punjabi winter, the bonfire goes up and the children go round the houses singing — and every child knows the song's first lines: Sundar mundriye, ho! Tera kaun vichara, ho! Dulla Bhatti wala, ho! Pretty girl, hey! Who will look out for you?","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.dulla-bhatti":{"route":"#/story/fk.dulla-bhatti","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dulla-bhatti:hook","title":"The Song Around the Lohri Fire","body":"Every January, around bonfires across Punjab, children sing a song to a man who lived four hundred years ago. This is who they are singing to.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.dulla-bhatti":{"route":"#/story/fk.dulla-bhatti","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dulla-bhatti:scenes:4:text","title":"The Song Around the Lohri Fire — the middle","body":"He declared them his daughters. His. A rescued girl might still be whispered about — but nobody whispers about the daughter of Dulla Bhatti. And when it was time, he arranged their weddings himself, standing as their father in front of everyone.","why":"A moment from The Song Around the Lohri Fire"},"sl-fk.dulla-bhatti":{"route":"#/story/fk.dulla-bhatti","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dulla-bhatti:moral","title":"What The Song Around the Lohri Fire says","body":"The strongest thing a strong person can do is make somebody safe — and call them family, out loud, in front of everyone.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.dulla-bhatti-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.dulla-bhatti","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dulla-bhatti:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Song Around the Lohri Fire","text":"गुड़","roman":"gud","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["jaggery","respect","a cloth band"],"a":0,"after":"गुड़ (gud) means “jaggery”."},"why":"A word from The Song Around the Lohri Fire"},"sp-fk.dulla-bhatti":{"route":"#/story/fk.dulla-bhatti","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dulla-bhatti:place:0","title":"The Song Around the Lohri Fire","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Punjab","Uttarakhand","Dadra & Nagar Haveli"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Punjab."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.sheikh-chilli-pot":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-pot","art":"art/story/fk-sheikh-chilli-pot.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-pot:scenes:0:text","title":"Sheikh Chilli Builds a Palace","body":"Sheikh Chilli — everyone in Punjab and Haryana knows Sheikh Chilli — was the greatest dreamer who ever lived. He never had two coins to rub together, but inside his head he was permanently, gloriously rich. His plans had plans.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.sheikh-chilli-pot":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-pot","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-pot:hook","title":"Sheikh Chilli Builds a Palace","body":"Sheikh Chilli was carrying a pot of oil to market when he began, in his head, to spend the money. It was going extremely well until his legs joined in.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.sheikh-chilli-pot":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-pot","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-pot:scenes:4:text","title":"Sheikh Chilli Builds a Palace — the middle","body":"\"Ah, but discipline!\" dream-Chilli told his dream-son sternly. \"Muddy hands off my new coat! When I say no—\" and here the real Sheikh Chilli, on the real road, gave a real stamp of his foot and a great sweep of his arm to show just how firmly a rich man says no.","why":"A moment from Sheikh Chilli Builds a Palace"},"sl-fk.sheikh-chilli-pot":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-pot","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-pot:moral","title":"What Sheikh Chilli Builds a Palace says","body":"Dream as tall as you like — but keep your hands on what you are actually carrying.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.sheikh-chilli-pot-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-pot","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-pot:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from Sheikh Chilli Builds a Palace","text":"मटका","roman":"matka","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["clay pot","respect","a cloth band"],"a":0,"after":"मटका (matka) means “clay pot”."},"why":"A word from Sheikh Chilli Builds a Palace"},"sp-fk.sheikh-chilli-pot":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-pot","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-pot:place:0","title":"Sheikh Chilli Builds a Palace","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Punjab","Bihar","Karnataka"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Punjab."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.sheikh-chilli-branch":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-branch","art":"art/story/fk-sheikh-chilli-branch.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-branch:scenes:0:text","title":"Sheikh Chilli and the Branch","body":"Sheikh Chilli needed firewood, and he needed it with the least possible effort, effort being a thing he saved carefully for emergencies. He picked a fine shady tree by the road, spotted a good thick branch, and climbed up with his saw between his teeth.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.sheikh-chilli-branch":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-branch","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-branch:hook","title":"Sheikh Chilli and the Branch","body":"Sheikh Chilli climbed a tree, sat down on a branch, and began to saw it off. There was one detail he had not fully considered.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.sheikh-chilli-branch":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-branch","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-branch:scenes:4:text","title":"Sheikh Chilli and the Branch — the middle","body":"Chilli sawed. The branch creaked. Chilli sawed on, humming.","why":"A moment from Sheikh Chilli and the Branch"},"sl-fk.sheikh-chilli-branch":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-branch","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-branch:moral","title":"What Sheikh Chilli and the Branch says","body":"Before you start cutting, check what you are sitting on. Most of wisdom is just looking.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-fk.sheikh-chilli-branch":{"route":"#/story/fk.sheikh-chilli-branch","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sheikh-chilli-branch:place:0","title":"Sheikh Chilli and the Branch","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Punjab","Gujarat","Madhya Pradesh"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Punjab."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.chiri-kaan":{"route":"#/story/fk.chiri-kaan","art":"art/story/fk-chiri-kaan.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.chiri-kaan:scenes:0:text","title":"The Sparrow, the Crow and the Khichdi","body":"Ik si chiri, ik si kaan — there was once a sparrow, there was once a crow. That is how the grandmothers of Punjab begin it, and every Punjabi child settles down at once, because they know exactly what is coming.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.chiri-kaan":{"route":"#/story/fk.chiri-kaan","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.chiri-kaan:hook","title":"The Sparrow, the Crow and the Khichdi","body":"A sparrow and a crow found one grain of rice and one grain of dal, and decided to cook khichdi together. \"Together\" turned out to need some discussion.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.chiri-kaan":{"route":"#/story/fk.chiri-kaan","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.chiri-kaan:scenes:4:text","title":"The Sparrow, the Crow and the Khichdi — the middle","body":"And then the khichdi was done, golden and steaming, and a remarkable medical miracle occurred: the crow's beak, wing and eyes all recovered in the same instant. \"Partner!\" he cried, hopping down with his plate. \"We have done it! Serve out our khichdi!\"","why":"A moment from The Sparrow, the Crow and the Khichdi"},"sl-fk.chiri-kaan":{"route":"#/story/fk.chiri-kaan","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.chiri-kaan:moral","title":"What The Sparrow, the Crow and the Khichdi says","body":"Share the eating with those who shared the working. And when your own beak conveniently aches — notice.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.chiri-kaan-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.chiri-kaan","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.chiri-kaan:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Sparrow, the Crow and the Khichdi","text":"खिचड़ी","roman":"khichdi","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["khichdi — rice and dal cooked together","shade","a wave"],"a":0,"after":"खिचड़ी (khichdi) means “khichdi — rice and dal cooked together”."},"why":"A word from The Sparrow, the Crow and the Khichdi"},"sp-fk.chiri-kaan":{"route":"#/story/fk.chiri-kaan","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.chiri-kaan:place:0","title":"The Sparrow, the Crow and the Khichdi","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Punjab","Ladakh","Telangana"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Punjab."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.raja-rasalu":{"route":"#/story/fk.raja-rasalu","art":"art/story/fk-raja-rasalu.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.raja-rasalu:scenes:0:text","title":"The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story","body":"Long ago in Sialkot, in old Punjab, a son was born to King Salvahan — and the wise men read the stars and gave a strange instruction: for twelve years, the boy must not be seen abroad. So Rasalu grew up behind walls, in a palace with everything in it except the world.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sh-fk.raja-rasalu":{"route":"#/story/fk.raja-rasalu","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.raja-rasalu:hook","title":"The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story","body":"Raja Rasalu grew up inside palace walls, forbidden to be seen. The day he finally rode out, Punjab got the hero its bards would sing for a thousand years.","why":"From Fields of Punjab and Haryana"},"sm-fk.raja-rasalu":{"route":"#/story/fk.raja-rasalu","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.raja-rasalu:scenes:4:text","title":"The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story — the middle","body":"For on the road, the singers say, Rasalu was forever stopping for small things. A trapped bird set loose. A creature in trouble helped out of it, while the parrot muttered about delays. \"Why bother?\" asks someone in nearly every ballad.","why":"A moment from The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story"},"sl-fk.raja-rasalu":{"route":"#/story/fk.raja-rasalu","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.raja-rasalu:moral","title":"What The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story says","body":"Set out when your story calls you — and be kind on the road. Every kindness travels ahead and waits for you.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.raja-rasalu-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.raja-rasalu","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.raja-rasalu:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story","text":"तोता","roman":"tota","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["parrot","rope","a horse"],"a":0,"after":"तोता (tota) means “parrot”."},"why":"A word from The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story"},"sw-fk.raja-rasalu-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.raja-rasalu","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.raja-rasalu:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story","text":"घोड़ा","roman":"ghoda","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["horse","search","a sorry"],"a":0,"after":"घोड़ा (ghoda) means “horse”."},"why":"A word from The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story"},"sp-fk.raja-rasalu":{"route":"#/story/fk.raja-rasalu","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.raja-rasalu:place:0","title":"The Prince Who Rode Out of the Story","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Punjab","Nagaland","Andhra Pradesh"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Punjab."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters":{"route":"#/story/fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters","art":"art/story/fk-ganga-yamuna-sisters.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters:scenes:0:text","title":"The Two Sisters Who Took Different Roads","body":"High in the mountains of Uttarakhand, closer together than you might think, two rivers are born. From the ice above Gangotri comes the Ganga. From the flanks of Bandarpunch, above Yamunotri, comes the Yamuna. One mountain neighbourhood; two beginnings.","why":"From Stories from the High Hills"},"sh-fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters":{"route":"#/story/fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters:hook","title":"The Two Sisters Who Took Different Roads","body":"Two rivers are born a mountain apart in Uttarakhand, take completely different roads through the world — and keep an appointment made at the beginning of time.","why":"From Stories from the High Hills"},"sm-fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters":{"route":"#/story/fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters:scenes:4:text","title":"The Two Sisters Who Took Different Roads — the middle","body":"For hundreds and hundreds of kilometres the two roads stay apart — so far apart that a child by one river might never think of the other. But the hill tellings smile at that, the way grandmothers smile at quarrels about distance.","why":"A moment from The Two Sisters Who Took Different Roads"},"sl-fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters":{"route":"#/story/fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters:moral","title":"What The Two Sisters Who Took Different Roads says","body":"Sisters may take roads a whole world apart. The meeting at the end is part of the family, too.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Two Sisters Who Took Different Roads","text":"संगम","roman":"sangam","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["the meeting of rivers","seven","a wave"],"a":0,"after":"संगम (sangam) means “the meeting of rivers”."},"why":"A word from The Two Sisters Who Took Different Roads"},"sp-fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters":{"route":"#/story/fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ganga-yamuna-sisters:place:0","title":"The Two Sisters Who Took Different Roads","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Uttarakhand","Tamil Nadu","Delhi"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Uttarakhand."},"why":"A map question"},"st-it.corbett":{"route":"#/story/it.corbett","art":"art/story/it-corbett.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.corbett:scenes:0:text","title":"The Hunter Who Put Down His Rifle","body":"Jim Corbett was born about a hundred and fifty years ago in Nainital, in the Kumaon hills of what is now Uttarakhand, and grew up half-wild himself — a boy who spent every possible hour in the forest, until he could read it like a book and speak its language of alarm calls and silences.","why":"From Stories from the High Hills"},"sh-it.corbett":{"route":"#/story/it.corbett","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.corbett:hook","title":"The Hunter Who Put Down His Rifle","body":"The most famous hunter in India spent the second half of his life fighting for the very animal he was famous for hunting. This is how a heart turns around.","why":"From Stories from the High Hills"},"sm-it.corbett":{"route":"#/story/it.corbett","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.corbett:scenes:4:text","title":"The Hunter Who Put Down His Rifle — the middle","body":"So the most famous hunter in the country faced a strange question: what should a man do with skills like his, in a world running out of tigers?","why":"A moment from The Hunter Who Put Down His Rifle"},"sl-it.corbett":{"route":"#/story/it.corbett","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.corbett:moral","title":"What The Hunter Who Put Down His Rifle says","body":"It is never too late to change what you aim at. The strongest people let what they learn turn them around.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-it.corbett":{"route":"#/story/it.corbett","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:it.corbett:place:0","title":"The Hunter Who Put Down His Rifle","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Uttarakhand","Assam","Jammu & Kashmir"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Uttarakhand."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.bugyal-rules":{"route":"#/story/fk.bugyal-rules","art":"art/story/fk-bugyal-rules.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bugyal-rules:scenes:0:text","title":"The Meadows Above the Trees","body":"Climb any big hill in Uttarakhand and the forest climbs with you — oak, then rhododendron, then deodar and fir — until, quite suddenly, the trees stop. Above that line the mountain wears open grass: rolling, cloud-brushed meadows called bugyals.","why":"From Stories from the High Hills"},"sh-fk.bugyal-rules":{"route":"#/story/fk.bugyal-rules","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bugyal-rules:hook","title":"The Meadows Above the Trees","body":"High above the last trees of Uttarakhand lie meadows so beautiful the hills say they belong to the gods. People may visit — but there are rules, and the rules are old.","why":"From Stories from the High Hills"},"sm-fk.bugyal-rules":{"route":"#/story/fk.bugyal-rules","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bugyal-rules:scenes:4:text","title":"The Meadows Above the Trees — the middle","body":"A boy going up for his first season with his grandfather's flock once asked the obvious question: do the gods really mind noise and mess?","why":"A moment from The Meadows Above the Trees"},"sl-fk.bugyal-rules":{"route":"#/story/fk.bugyal-rules","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bugyal-rules:moral","title":"What The Meadows Above the Trees says","body":"The oldest rules of respect are often care in disguise. Keep them, and the beautiful places keep themselves.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.bugyal-rules-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.bugyal-rules","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bugyal-rules:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Meadows Above the Trees","text":"घास","roman":"ghaas","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["grass","search","a sorry"],"a":0,"after":"घास (ghaas) means “grass”."},"why":"A word from The Meadows Above the Trees"},"sw-fk.bugyal-rules-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.bugyal-rules","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bugyal-rules:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Meadows Above the Trees","text":"बादल","roman":"baadal","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["cloud","sickness","an axe"],"a":0,"after":"बादल (baadal) means “cloud”."},"why":"A word from The Meadows Above the Trees"},"sp-fk.bugyal-rules":{"route":"#/story/fk.bugyal-rules","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bugyal-rules:place:0","title":"The Meadows Above the Trees","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Uttarakhand","Goa","Manipur"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Uttarakhand."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.nanda-raj-jat":{"route":"#/story/fk.nanda-raj-jat","art":"art/story/fk-nanda-raj-jat.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.nanda-raj-jat:scenes:0:text","title":"Walking the Goddess Home","body":"Over Garhwal and Kumaon stands Nanda Devi — one of the highest mountains in India, and much more than a mountain. To the hill people she is Nanda, the goddess — and, tenderly, she is also the hills' own daughter, married into the high snows.","why":"From Stories from the High Hills"},"sh-fk.nanda-raj-jat":{"route":"#/story/fk.nanda-raj-jat","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.nanda-raj-jat:hook","title":"Walking the Goddess Home","body":"Once in a generation, the hills of Uttarakhand walk their beloved goddess home to her mountain — for weeks, on foot, behind a four-horned ram.","why":"From Stories from the High Hills"},"sm-fk.nanda-raj-jat":{"route":"#/story/fk.nanda-raj-jat","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.nanda-raj-jat:scenes:4:text","title":"Walking the Goddess Home — the middle","body":"Every halt is a homecoming. Villages on the route host the goddess overnight as family — songs, lamps, tears, festival food — because she is not passing through their village. She is theirs, visiting, one last night before the long snows.","why":"A moment from Walking the Goddess Home"},"sl-fk.nanda-raj-jat":{"route":"#/story/fk.nanda-raj-jat","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.nanda-raj-jat:moral","title":"What Walking the Goddess Home says","body":"The deepest festivals are ordinary feelings made enormous — every family's farewell, walked up a mountain together.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.nanda-raj-jat-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.nanda-raj-jat","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.nanda-raj-jat:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from Walking the Goddess Home","text":"विदाई","roman":"vidaai","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["farewell, send-off","snake","assembly hall"],"a":0,"after":"विदाई (vidaai) means “farewell, send-off”."},"why":"A word from Walking the Goddess Home"},"sp-fk.nanda-raj-jat":{"route":"#/story/fk.nanda-raj-jat","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.nanda-raj-jat:place:0","title":"Walking the Goddess Home","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Uttarakhand","Kerala","Rajasthan"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Uttarakhand."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.cheraman-sails":{"route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-sails","art":"art/story/fk-cheraman-sails.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.cheraman-sails:scenes:0:text","title":"The Sailors Who Found the Islands","body":"Off the coast of Kerala, scattered over the blue like a handful of green beads, lie the islands of Lakshadweep — the smallest of all the places on India's map, and one of the loveliest. The islanders themselves tell a story about how anyone ever found them at all.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.cheraman-sails":{"route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-sails","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.cheraman-sails:hook","title":"The Sailors Who Found the Islands","body":"They set out to look for a king who had sailed away. They found something better than a king.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.cheraman-sails":{"route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-sails","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.cheraman-sails:scenes:4:text","title":"The Sailors Who Found the Islands — the middle","body":"When the wind finally dropped, they were wrecked and drifting, out of fresh water, with no land in sight in any direction at all.","why":"A moment from The Sailors Who Found the Islands"},"sl-fk.cheraman-sails":{"route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-sails","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.cheraman-sails:moral","title":"What The Sailors Who Found the Islands says","body":"Set out looking for one thing and stay ready to find another. The best discoveries are usually the ones you were not making.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.cheraman-sails-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-sails","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.cheraman-sails:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Sailors Who Found the Islands","text":"खोज","roman":"khoj","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["search","sickness","an axe"],"a":0,"after":"खोज (khoj) means “search”."},"why":"A word from The Sailors Who Found the Islands"},"sp-fk.cheraman-sails":{"route":"#/story/fk.cheraman-sails","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.cheraman-sails:place:0","title":"The Sailors Who Found the Islands","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Lakshadweep","Nagaland","Andaman & Nicobar Islands"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Lakshadweep."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.ubaidullah-lamp":{"route":"#/story/fk.ubaidullah-lamp","art":"art/story/fk-ubaidullah-lamp.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ubaidullah-lamp:scenes:0:text","title":"The Dream That Crossed the Sea","body":"The people of Lakshadweep are Muslim, and have been for a very long time — the mosque is the middle of every island, the day is shaped by the five prayers, and the boats go out after the morning one.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.ubaidullah-lamp":{"route":"#/story/fk.ubaidullah-lamp","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ubaidullah-lamp:hook","title":"The Dream That Crossed the Sea","body":"A man had a dream that told him to sail east. The sea took everything he had except the dream.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.ubaidullah-lamp":{"route":"#/story/fk.ubaidullah-lamp","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ubaidullah-lamp:scenes:4:text","title":"The Dream That Crossed the Sea — the middle","body":"It was Amini, in the islands we now call Lakshadweep. The islanders found a stranger on their sand at dawn with nothing at all — no goods, no gold, not even shoes. They gave him water, and food, and shade, because that is what you do.","why":"A moment from The Dream That Crossed the Sea"},"sl-fk.ubaidullah-lamp":{"route":"#/story/fk.ubaidullah-lamp","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ubaidullah-lamp:moral","title":"What The Dream That Crossed the Sea says","body":"A message carried gently travels further than one carried loudly. He arrived with nothing, and it was enough.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-fk.ubaidullah-lamp":{"route":"#/story/fk.ubaidullah-lamp","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ubaidullah-lamp:place:0","title":"The Dream That Crossed the Sea","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Lakshadweep","Tamil Nadu","Daman & Diu"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Lakshadweep."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.odam-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.odam-race","art":"art/story/fk-odam-race.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.odam-race:scenes:0:text","title":"Race Day on the Lagoon","body":"On Minicoy, the southernmost island of Lakshadweep, every village keeps a great racing boat, long and slim and polished like a musical instrument.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.odam-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.odam-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.odam-race:hook","title":"Race Day on the Lagoon","body":"Ten rowers, one drum, and a whole island on the beach shouting. Today the boats race.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.odam-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.odam-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.odam-race:scenes:4:text","title":"Race Day on the Lagoon — the middle","body":"This year, one crew has a problem. Their strongest rower has hurt his hand.","why":"A moment from Race Day on the Lagoon"},"sl-fk.odam-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.odam-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.odam-race:moral","title":"What Race Day on the Lagoon says","body":"Ten rowing as one beat eleven rowing as eleven — on the lagoon and everywhere else.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-fk.odam-race":{"route":"#/story/fk.odam-race","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.odam-race:place:0","title":"Race Day on the Lagoon","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Lakshadweep","Arunachal Pradesh","Jharkhand"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Lakshadweep."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.minicoy-mahl":{"route":"#/story/fk.minicoy-mahl","art":"art/story/fk-minicoy-mahl.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.minicoy-mahl:scenes:0:text","title":"The Island That Speaks Mahl","body":"Most of the islands of Lakshadweep speak Malayalam, like the Kerala coast they came from. But sail south, past all the others, to the last island — Minicoy — and the language changes. Here the people speak Mahl.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.minicoy-mahl":{"route":"#/story/fk.minicoy-mahl","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.minicoy-mahl:hook","title":"The Island That Speaks Mahl","body":"On one island of Lakshadweep, the words come from across the sea — and are written from right to left.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.minicoy-mahl":{"route":"#/story/fk.minicoy-mahl","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.minicoy-mahl:scenes:4:text","title":"The Island That Speaks Mahl — the middle","body":"And Mahl has one more surprise. It is written in its own script, called Thaana — and Thaana runs from right to left across the page, like Arabic.","why":"A moment from The Island That Speaks Mahl"},"sl-fk.minicoy-mahl":{"route":"#/story/fk.minicoy-mahl","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.minicoy-mahl:moral","title":"What The Island That Speaks Mahl says","body":"A border is a line on the water. A language is a family — and family reaches across.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.minicoy-mahl-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.minicoy-mahl","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.minicoy-mahl:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Island That Speaks Mahl","text":"पड़ोसी","roman":"padosi","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["neighbour","teacher","broom"],"a":0,"after":"पड़ोसी (padosi) means “neighbour”."},"why":"A word from The Island That Speaks Mahl"},"sw-fk.minicoy-mahl-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.minicoy-mahl","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.minicoy-mahl:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Island That Speaks Mahl","text":"चचेरा भाई","roman":"chachera bhai","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["cousin","tiger","cleverness"],"a":0,"after":"चचेरा भाई (chachera bhai) means “cousin”."},"why":"A word from The Island That Speaks Mahl"},"sp-fk.minicoy-mahl":{"route":"#/story/fk.minicoy-mahl","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.minicoy-mahl:place:0","title":"The Island That Speaks Mahl","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Lakshadweep","Dadra & Nagar Haveli","Manipur"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Lakshadweep."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.tuna-star":{"route":"#/story/fk.tuna-star","art":"art/story/fk-tuna-star.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tuna-star:scenes:0:text","title":"One Fish at a Time","body":"Before first light on a Lakshadweep island, the tuna boat is already awake. The crew prays the dawn prayer, drinks something hot, and goes down to the water while the sky is still deciding what colour to be.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.tuna-star":{"route":"#/story/fk.tuna-star","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tuna-star:hook","title":"One Fish at a Time","body":"No nets. No dragging the sea empty. On these islands, tuna are caught one at a time, like a fair game — and a star shows the way home.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.tuna-star":{"route":"#/story/fk.tuna-star","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tuna-star:scenes:4:text","title":"One Fish at a Time — the middle","body":"A visitor once asked the captain the obvious question: why not use a giant net and take the whole school in one go?","why":"A moment from One Fish at a Time"},"sl-fk.tuna-star":{"route":"#/story/fk.tuna-star","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tuna-star:moral","title":"What One Fish at a Time says","body":"Take what you need and leave the sea full. And always know your still star.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.tuna-star-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.tuna-star","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tuna-star:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from One Fish at a Time","text":"तारा","roman":"tara","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["star","stream","blue"],"a":0,"after":"तारा (tara) means “star”."},"why":"A word from One Fish at a Time"},"sp-fk.tuna-star":{"route":"#/story/fk.tuna-star","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tuna-star:place:0","title":"One Fish at a Time","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Lakshadweep","Karnataka","Rajasthan"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Lakshadweep."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.first-coconut":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-coconut","art":"art/story/fk-first-coconut.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-coconut:scenes:0:text","title":"The Nut That Crossed the Ocean","body":"Here is a tale for the smallest islands — the ones that began as nothing but a white sandbank on a coral reef, far out at sea, with no tree, no shade, no fresh water, and nobody home.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.first-coconut":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-coconut","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-coconut:hook","title":"The Nut That Crossed the Ocean","body":"Before there was anyone on the sandbank, something small and round and patient came riding in on the waves.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.first-coconut":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-coconut","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-coconut:scenes:4:text","title":"The Nut That Crossed the Ocean — the middle","body":"And at last a wave, taller than the rest, picked it up and set it down high on the white sandbank in the middle of the sea — above the tide line, in the warm sand, in the one place in a thousand miles where it could begin.","why":"A moment from The Nut That Crossed the Ocean"},"sl-fk.first-coconut":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-coconut","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-coconut:moral","title":"What The Nut That Crossed the Ocean says","body":"Small, slow and packed with patience will cross an ocean that big and loud cannot.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.first-coconut-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-coconut","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-coconut:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from The Nut That Crossed the Ocean","text":"नारियल","roman":"nariyal","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["coconut","the beat","cage"],"a":0,"after":"नारियल (nariyal) means “coconut”."},"why":"A word from The Nut That Crossed the Ocean"},"sp-fk.first-coconut":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-coconut","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-coconut:place:0","title":"The Nut That Crossed the Ocean","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Lakshadweep","Mizoram","West Bengal"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Lakshadweep."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.turtle-lagoon":{"route":"#/story/fk.turtle-lagoon","art":"art/story/fk-turtle-lagoon.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.turtle-lagoon:scenes:0:text","title":"The Turtle Who Kept the Lagoon Calm","body":"Every Lakshadweep island has two seas. Outside the reef is the big dark ocean, which is always in a hurry and always shouting. Inside the reef is the lagoon — green glass, warm as bathwater, so still you can watch a crab walk on the bottom from a boat.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.turtle-lagoon":{"route":"#/story/fk.turtle-lagoon","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.turtle-lagoon:hook","title":"The Turtle Who Kept the Lagoon Calm","body":"Outside the reef, the ocean roars all day. Inside, the water lies still as a mirror. The islanders' children know who to thank.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.turtle-lagoon":{"route":"#/story/fk.turtle-lagoon","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.turtle-lagoon:scenes:4:text","title":"The Turtle Who Kept the Lagoon Calm — the middle","body":"They watched from the beach. Out in the churning lagoon, the old turtle was swimming her circle — the same circle, at the same speed, unhurried as sunrise, while the water leapt and fussed around her.","why":"A moment from The Turtle Who Kept the Lagoon Calm"},"sl-fk.turtle-lagoon":{"route":"#/story/fk.turtle-lagoon","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.turtle-lagoon:moral","title":"What The Turtle Who Kept the Lagoon Calm says","body":"You cannot shout a storm quiet. But calm, kept steadily enough, is catching.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-fk.turtle-lagoon":{"route":"#/story/fk.turtle-lagoon","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.turtle-lagoon:place:0","title":"The Turtle Who Kept the Lagoon Calm","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Lakshadweep","Telangana","Chhattisgarh"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Lakshadweep."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.wandering-sandbank":{"route":"#/story/fk.wandering-sandbank","art":"art/story/fk-wandering-sandbank.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.wandering-sandbank:scenes:0:text","title":"The Sandbank That Goes Visiting","body":"Near one of the islands of Lakshadweep there is a sandbank — a little bare hill of white sand out in the lagoon, with no trees, no huts, no anything. On some days you can row out, land on it, and have the smallest country in the world all to yourself.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.wandering-sandbank":{"route":"#/story/fk.wandering-sandbank","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.wandering-sandbank:hook","title":"The Sandbank That Goes Visiting","body":"Some mornings there is a little white island in the lagoon. Some mornings there is only sea. The children say it goes visiting.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.wandering-sandbank":{"route":"#/story/fk.wandering-sandbank","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.wandering-sandbank:scenes:4:text","title":"The Sandbank That Goes Visiting — the middle","body":"And who pulls the tide? Look up. The moon, all that way away, tugs at the ocean as it passes, and the ocean leans towards it the way sunflowers lean at the sun. The moon moves the sea; the sea covers the sandbank.","why":"A moment from The Sandbank That Goes Visiting"},"sl-fk.wandering-sandbank":{"route":"#/story/fk.wandering-sandbank","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.wandering-sandbank:moral","title":"What The Sandbank That Goes Visiting says","body":"The sea keeps its own timetable. The wise thing is not to fight it — it is to know it.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.wandering-sandbank-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.wandering-sandbank","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.wandering-sandbank:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Sandbank That Goes Visiting","text":"चाँद","roman":"chand","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["moon","the proper limit","cart"],"a":0,"after":"चाँद (chand) means “moon”."},"why":"A word from The Sandbank That Goes Visiting"},"sw-fk.wandering-sandbank-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.wandering-sandbank","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.wandering-sandbank:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Sandbank That Goes Visiting","text":"ज्वार","roman":"jwaar","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["tide","tiger","cleverness"],"a":0,"after":"ज्वार (jwaar) means “tide”."},"why":"A word from The Sandbank That Goes Visiting"},"sp-fk.wandering-sandbank":{"route":"#/story/fk.wandering-sandbank","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.wandering-sandbank:place:0","title":"The Sandbank That Goes Visiting","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Lakshadweep","Andhra Pradesh","Haryana"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Lakshadweep."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.tree-that-counts":{"route":"#/story/fk.tree-that-counts","art":"art/story/fk-tree-that-counts.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tree-that-counts:scenes:0:text","title":"The Trees That Count as Family","body":"South of the Andamans, strung towards Sumatra, lie the Nicobar Islands — and they belong, first and always, to the Nicobarese, who have lived there for many centuries and still do.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.tree-that-counts":{"route":"#/story/fk.tree-that-counts","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tree-that-counts:hook","title":"The Trees That Count as Family","body":"In the Nicobar Islands, when a baby is born, somebody plants a tree — and the tree is not a decoration. It is a plan.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.tree-that-counts":{"route":"#/story/fk.tree-that-counts","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tree-that-counts:scenes:4:text","title":"The Trees That Count as Family — the middle","body":"A visitor once wondered aloud why anyone would measure wealth in trees when coins are so much easier to count.","why":"A moment from The Trees That Count as Family"},"sl-fk.tree-that-counts":{"route":"#/story/fk.tree-that-counts","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tree-that-counts:moral","title":"What The Trees That Count as Family says","body":"Real wealth is whatever still feeds your family in fifty years. Count in trees, not coins.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-fk.tree-that-counts":{"route":"#/story/fk.tree-that-counts","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.tree-that-counts:place:0","title":"The Trees That Count as Family","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Andaman & Nicobar Islands","Dadra & Nagar Haveli","Meghalaya"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Andaman & Nicobar Islands."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.ross-ficus":{"route":"#/story/fk.ross-ficus","art":"art/story/fk-ross-ficus.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ross-ficus:scenes:0:text","title":"The Island the Roots Took Back","body":"Just off Port Blair in the Andamans lies a tiny island you can walk around in an hour. A hundred and some years ago, under British rule, it was the little capital of the whole island chain — Ross Island, where the officers who governed lived with their families.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.ross-ficus":{"route":"#/story/fk.ross-ficus","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ross-ficus:hook","title":"The Island the Roots Took Back","body":"It had ballrooms, a bakery, a printing press and a church. Then the people left — and the forest quietly moved back in.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.ross-ficus":{"route":"#/story/fk.ross-ficus","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ross-ficus:scenes:4:text","title":"The Island the Roots Took Back — the middle","body":"Go there today and you will see something few places on Earth can show you: whole buildings held in the arms of trees. Ficus roots pour down over doorways like slow waterfalls, wrap window arches, climb the church tower.","why":"A moment from The Island the Roots Took Back"},"sl-fk.ross-ficus":{"route":"#/story/fk.ross-ficus","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ross-ficus:moral","title":"What The Island the Roots Took Back says","body":"What is built by force stands for a while. What grows by patience takes it back.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.ross-ficus-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.ross-ficus","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ross-ficus:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from The Island the Roots Took Back","text":"दीवार","roman":"deewar","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["wall","to hide","colour"],"a":0,"after":"दीवार (deewar) means “wall”."},"why":"A word from The Island the Roots Took Back"},"sp-fk.ross-ficus":{"route":"#/story/fk.ross-ficus","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.ross-ficus:place:0","title":"The Island the Roots Took Back","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Andaman & Nicobar Islands","Karnataka","Puducherry"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Andaman & Nicobar Islands."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.north-bay-light":{"route":"#/story/fk.north-bay-light","art":"art/story/fk-north-bay-light.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.north-bay-light:scenes:0:text","title":"The Lighthouse That Says Its Name","body":"At the mouth of the harbour near Port Blair, on a green headland called North Bay, stands a lighthouse — white and red-banded, above water so clear that the coral gardens below it are famous across India.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.north-bay-light":{"route":"#/story/fk.north-bay-light","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.north-bay-light:hook","title":"The Lighthouse That Says Its Name","body":"Every lighthouse on every coast blinks — but no two blink alike. Each one is saying who it is, all night long.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.north-bay-light":{"route":"#/story/fk.north-bay-light","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.north-bay-light:scenes:4:text","title":"The Lighthouse That Says Its Name — the middle","body":"The keeper's work is steadiness itself: the lamp, the lens, the log book. It is not exciting, and it is not supposed to be. Somewhere out in the dark, someone he will never meet is safe because the light did tonight exactly what it did last night.","why":"A moment from The Lighthouse That Says Its Name"},"sl-fk.north-bay-light":{"route":"#/story/fk.north-bay-light","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.north-bay-light:moral","title":"What The Lighthouse That Says Its Name says","body":"Anyone can shine once. A lighthouse is trusted because it shines the same, all night, every night.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.north-bay-light-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.north-bay-light","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.north-bay-light:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Lighthouse That Says Its Name","text":"जहाज़","roman":"jahaaz","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["ship","tree","craftsperson"],"a":0,"after":"जहाज़ (jahaaz) means “ship”."},"why":"A word from The Lighthouse That Says Its Name"},"sp-fk.north-bay-light":{"route":"#/story/fk.north-bay-light","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.north-bay-light:place:0","title":"The Lighthouse That Says Its Name","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Andaman & Nicobar Islands","Madhya Pradesh","Uttar Pradesh"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Andaman & Nicobar Islands."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.dugong-meadow":{"route":"#/story/fk.dugong-meadow","art":"art/story/fk-dugong-meadow.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dugong-meadow:scenes:0:text","title":"The Dugong Who Remembered","body":"Most people know the sea has fish. Fewer know the sea has meadows — real ones, of soft green seagrass, growing in the shallow sunlit water of quiet bays. And where there are meadows, there are grazers. In the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, the grazer is the dugong: the sea cow.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.dugong-meadow":{"route":"#/story/fk.dugong-meadow","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dugong-meadow:hook","title":"The Dugong Who Remembered","body":"Under the sea there are meadows, and in the meadows grazes a gentle giant with a very long memory.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.dugong-meadow":{"route":"#/story/fk.dugong-meadow","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dugong-meadow:scenes:4:text","title":"The Dugong Who Remembered — the middle","body":"The old one nudged her and turned away from the ruin, out along the coast. She was remembering: three bays north, past the point where the mangroves lean out, there is another meadow. Her own mother had shown her, long ago, on a day she had never needed until now.","why":"A moment from The Dugong Who Remembered"},"sl-fk.dugong-meadow":{"route":"#/story/fk.dugong-meadow","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dugong-meadow:moral","title":"What The Dugong Who Remembered says","body":"The elders carry the map of the spare meadows. Learn it before you need it.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-fk.dugong-meadow":{"route":"#/story/fk.dugong-meadow","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.dugong-meadow:place:0","title":"The Dugong Who Remembered","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Andaman & Nicobar Islands","Sikkim","Chhattisgarh"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Andaman & Nicobar Islands."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.flying-fox-ferry":{"route":"#/story/fk.flying-fox-ferry","art":"art/story/fk-flying-fox-ferry.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.flying-fox-ferry:scenes:0:text","title":"The Evening Ferry of the Flying Foxes","body":"On the islands of the Andamans, the day ends with a show, and the show is free. Just as the sky goes gold, a long, slow, flapping line rises out of the tall trees and sets off across the water — hundreds of big dark shapes, unhurried as a ferry queue.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.flying-fox-ferry":{"route":"#/story/fk.flying-fox-ferry","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.flying-fox-ferry:hook","title":"The Evening Ferry of the Flying Foxes","body":"Every evening, on schedule, a slow dark river crosses the island sky. It is not birds. Look closer.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.flying-fox-ferry":{"route":"#/story/fk.flying-fox-ferry","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.flying-fox-ferry:scenes:4:text","title":"The Evening Ferry of the Flying Foxes — the middle","body":"\"Watch the ferry,\" said his aunt. \"Every fox that eats a fig carries its seeds away inside her, and somewhere out over the islands tonight, seeds will fall — into clearings, onto beaches, across channels no seed could swim.","why":"A moment from The Evening Ferry of the Flying Foxes"},"sl-fk.flying-fox-ferry":{"route":"#/story/fk.flying-fox-ferry","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.flying-fox-ferry:moral","title":"What The Evening Ferry of the Flying Foxes says","body":"Some of the most important work in the world looks like a slow line of somebody else's evening.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.flying-fox-ferry-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.flying-fox-ferry","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.flying-fox-ferry:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Evening Ferry of the Flying Foxes","text":"बीज","roman":"beej","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["seed","tree","craftsperson"],"a":0,"after":"बीज (beej) means “seed”."},"why":"A word from The Evening Ferry of the Flying Foxes"},"sp-fk.flying-fox-ferry":{"route":"#/story/fk.flying-fox-ferry","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.flying-fox-ferry:place:0","title":"The Evening Ferry of the Flying Foxes","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Andaman & Nicobar Islands","Andhra Pradesh","Haryana"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Andaman & Nicobar Islands."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.one-island-bird":{"route":"#/story/fk.one-island-bird","art":"art/story/fk-one-island-bird.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.one-island-bird:scenes:0:text","title":"The Bird With One Island","body":"Far out beyond the main Andaman Islands, alone in the open sea, stands a small green volcanic island called Narcondam. It has cliffs, forest, no town, and one resident that has made it famous among people who love birds: the Narcondam hornbill.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.one-island-bird":{"route":"#/story/fk.one-island-bird","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.one-island-bird:hook","title":"The Bird With One Island","body":"Every kind of bird lives somewhere. One hornbill lives on exactly one small island — and nowhere else on Earth.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.one-island-bird":{"route":"#/story/fk.one-island-bird","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.one-island-bird:scenes:4:text","title":"The Bird With One Island — the middle","body":"Which leaves one obvious problem: a walled-in mother cannot go shopping.","why":"A moment from The Bird With One Island"},"sl-fk.one-island-bird":{"route":"#/story/fk.one-island-bird","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.one-island-bird:moral","title":"What The Bird With One Island says","body":"A treasure that exists in only one place makes that place its keeper.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-fk.one-island-bird":{"route":"#/story/fk.one-island-bird","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.one-island-bird:place:0","title":"The Bird With One Island","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Andaman & Nicobar Islands","Delhi","Maharashtra"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Andaman & Nicobar Islands."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.island-of-tongues":{"route":"#/story/fk.island-of-tongues","art":"art/story/fk-island-of-tongues.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.island-of-tongues:scenes:0:text","title":"The Bazaar of Many Tongues","body":"Go to the morning market in Port Blair — the main town of the Andamans, lately given the new official name Sri Vijaya Puram — and just listen. Bengali at the fish stall. Tamil at the flower stall.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.island-of-tongues":{"route":"#/story/fk.island-of-tongues","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.island-of-tongues:hook","title":"The Bazaar of Many Tongues","body":"Stand in one market in the Andamans and close your eyes. You can hear half of India — and something far, far older.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.island-of-tongues":{"route":"#/story/fk.island-of-tongues","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.island-of-tongues:scenes:4:text","title":"The Bazaar of Many Tongues — the middle","body":"Some of those ancient voices have grown very quiet. The languages of the Great Andamanese peoples, once many, now have only a handful of speakers, and elders have worked with linguists to write down words, songs and names so that what remains is kept with honour.","why":"A moment from The Bazaar of Many Tongues"},"sl-fk.island-of-tongues":{"route":"#/story/fk.island-of-tongues","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.island-of-tongues:moral","title":"What The Bazaar of Many Tongues says","body":"Every language is somebody's home. Visit the ones you are invited into; guard the ones you are not.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-fk.island-of-tongues":{"route":"#/story/fk.island-of-tongues","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.island-of-tongues:place:0","title":"The Bazaar of Many Tongues","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Andaman & Nicobar Islands","Jammu & Kashmir","Punjab"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Andaman & Nicobar Islands."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.first-dance":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-dance","art":"art/story/fk-first-dance.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-dance:scenes:0:text","title":"How the First Evening Got Its Dance","body":"The Santhals of Jharkhand sing their whole history, from the very beginning of the world — and this is one of the tellings from early in that story, after the first ancestors, when their children and grandchildren had villages, and fields, and everything a people needs.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.first-dance":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-dance","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-dance:hook","title":"How the First Evening Got Its Dance","body":"The world was made, the fields were planted, the day's work was done — and then everyone just stood there. Something was missing.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.first-dance":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-dance","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-dance:scenes:4:text","title":"How the First Evening Got Its Dance — the middle","body":"Then came the question that made it Santhal. One dancer alone in the middle looked wonderful and a little lonely. Everyone wanted to join — but how do many people dance together without becoming a herd of collisions?","why":"A moment from How the First Evening Got Its Dance"},"sl-fk.first-dance":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-dance","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-dance:moral","title":"What How the First Evening Got Its Dance says","body":"A line of linked hands has no collisions and no end. Villages that dance together hold together.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sp-fk.first-dance":{"route":"#/story/fk.first-dance","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.first-dance:place:0","title":"How the First Evening Got Its Dance","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jharkhand","Manipur","Uttarakhand"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jharkhand."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.singbonga-furnaces":{"route":"#/story/fk.singbonga-furnaces","art":"art/story/fk-singbonga-furnaces.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.singbonga-furnaces:scenes:0:text","title":"Sing Bonga and the Smoke That Hid the Sky","body":"The Munda people of Jharkhand, and their neighbours the Asur — who were master iron-smelters for centuries, real ones, whose old furnace mounds still dot the plateau — tell a story from the deep beginning of things, about iron, smoke, and the sun himself.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.singbonga-furnaces":{"route":"#/story/fk.singbonga-furnaces","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.singbonga-furnaces:hook","title":"Sing Bonga and the Smoke That Hid the Sky","body":"The iron furnaces burned day and night, and the smoke rose and rose, until even the sun had to do something about it.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.singbonga-furnaces":{"route":"#/story/fk.singbonga-furnaces","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.singbonga-furnaces:scenes:4:text","title":"Sing Bonga and the Smoke That Hid the Sky — the middle","body":"One after another the birds flew down to the furnaces to carry the message — and came back changed. The tellings say the king crow came back scorched black as charcoal, and others came back marked by smoke and cinders — and that is why those birds wear those colours to this day.","why":"A moment from Sing Bonga and the Smoke That Hid the Sky"},"sl-fk.singbonga-furnaces":{"route":"#/story/fk.singbonga-furnaces","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.singbonga-furnaces:moral","title":"What Sing Bonga and the Smoke That Hid the Sky says","body":"Make your fine things — but let the fires rest. Even the sun sets every evening.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.singbonga-furnaces-0":{"route":"#/story/fk.singbonga-furnaces","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.singbonga-furnaces:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from Sing Bonga and the Smoke That Hid the Sky","text":"सूरज","roman":"sooraj","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["sun","waterfall","devotion"],"a":0,"after":"सूरज (sooraj) means “sun”."},"why":"A word from Sing Bonga and the Smoke That Hid the Sky"},"sp-fk.singbonga-furnaces":{"route":"#/story/fk.singbonga-furnaces","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.singbonga-furnaces:place:0","title":"Sing Bonga and the Smoke That Hid the Sky","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jharkhand","Rajasthan","Bihar"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jharkhand."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.sal-grove-promise":{"route":"#/story/fk.sal-grove-promise","art":"art/story/fk-sal-grove-promise.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sal-grove-promise:scenes:0:text","title":"The Grove the Axes Never Touch","body":"Drive through Jharkhand and you will see it again and again: open fields, houses, roads — and then, beside a village, a patch of tall old forest standing untouched, dark green and quiet, like a piece of the original world.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.sal-grove-promise":{"route":"#/story/fk.sal-grove-promise","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sal-grove-promise:hook","title":"The Grove the Axes Never Touch","body":"When the first fields were cleared, the trees fell one by one. Except in one place — and there is a reason the axes stop there.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.sal-grove-promise":{"route":"#/story/fk.sal-grove-promise","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sal-grove-promise:scenes:4:text","title":"The Grove the Axes Never Touch — the middle","body":"This is the heart of the Sarna way — the faith of many Adivasi communities of Jharkhand, who honour a single great spirit and the spirits of grove, hill and stream, and who will tell you plainly: our temple has a roof of leaves.","why":"A moment from The Grove the Axes Never Touch"},"sl-fk.sal-grove-promise":{"route":"#/story/fk.sal-grove-promise","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sal-grove-promise:moral","title":"What The Grove the Axes Never Touch says","body":"Take what you need and keep a grove whole. The thank-you you leave standing feeds your grandchildren.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.sal-grove-promise-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.sal-grove-promise","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sal-grove-promise:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Grove the Axes Never Touch","text":"छाया","roman":"chhaya","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["shade","wooden sandals","egg"],"a":0,"after":"छाया (chhaya) means “shade”."},"why":"A word from The Grove the Axes Never Touch"},"sp-fk.sal-grove-promise":{"route":"#/story/fk.sal-grove-promise","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sal-grove-promise:place:0","title":"The Grove the Axes Never Touch","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jharkhand","West Bengal","Gujarat"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jharkhand."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.karam-brothers":{"route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers","art":"art/story/fk-karam-brothers.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:scenes:0:text","title":"The Branch the Brothers Brought Home","body":"Every year, when the rains are ripening the rice, the villages of Jharkhand keep the festival of Karam. A branch of the karam tree is carried home with songs and drums, planted with honour in the courtyard, and danced around all night.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.karam-brothers":{"route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:hook","title":"The Branch the Brothers Brought Home","body":"Two brothers had good luck and did not know where it came from. Then they found out — the hard way, and then the walking way.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.karam-brothers":{"route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:scenes:4:text","title":"The Branch the Brothers Brought Home — the middle","body":"They asked an elder what they had broken. \"You know what you have broken,\" she said. \"The question is what you will do about it.\"","why":"A moment from The Branch the Brothers Brought Home"},"sl-fk.karam-brothers":{"route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:moral","title":"What The Branch the Brothers Brought Home says","body":"Luck is a guest. It stays where it is greeted — and once neglected, it must be fetched back on foot.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.karam-brothers-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Branch the Brothers Brought Home","text":"त्योहार","roman":"tyohaar","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["festival","a friend","five"],"a":0,"after":"त्योहार (tyohaar) means “festival”."},"why":"A word from The Branch the Brothers Brought Home"},"sp-fk.karam-brothers":{"route":"#/story/fk.karam-brothers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.karam-brothers:place:0","title":"The Branch the Brothers Brought Home","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jharkhand","Chhattisgarh","Lakshadweep"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jharkhand."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.bamboo-bonga":{"route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga","art":"art/story/fk-bamboo-bonga.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:scenes:0:text","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","body":"In the Santhal way of speaking, the living world is full of bongas — the spirits of places: a spirit of the hill, of the stream, of the old tree at the crossing. A place with a bonga is a place that is somebody, not just something.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.bamboo-bonga":{"route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:hook","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","body":"The bamboo grove fed him every winter. One winter he decided to eat all of it at once — and something in the grove cleared its throat.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.bamboo-bonga":{"route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:scenes:4:text","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo — the middle","body":"\"You have eaten here every winter of your life,\" said the keeper of the grove. \"Who fed you last winter?\" \"This grove,\" said the elephant. \"And the winter before?\" \"This grove.\" \"And who will feed you NEXT winter?\"","why":"A moment from The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo"},"sl-fk.bamboo-bonga":{"route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:moral","title":"What The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo says","body":"Take from the edges and spare the heart, and the grove will outlast your grandchildren's hunger.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.bamboo-bonga-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","text":"कल","roman":"kal","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["tomorrow","wooden sandals","egg"],"a":0,"after":"कल (kal) means “tomorrow”."},"why":"A word from The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo"},"sp-fk.bamboo-bonga":{"route":"#/story/fk.bamboo-bonga","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.bamboo-bonga:place:0","title":"The Elephant and the Keeper of the Bamboo","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jharkhand","Haryana","Odisha"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jharkhand."},"why":"A map question"},"st-fk.sarhul-flowers":{"route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers","art":"art/story/fk-sarhul-flowers.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:scenes:0:text","title":"When the Sal Trees Bloom","body":"Every spring, the sal forests of Jharkhand do something quietly spectacular: the great trees flower — small, cream-pale blossoms by the million, and a honeyed scent that drifts for miles. And when the sal blooms, the Adivasi new year has arrived.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sh-fk.sarhul-flowers":{"route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:hook","title":"When the Sal Trees Bloom","body":"In Jharkhand, the new year does not arrive by calendar. It arrives by flower — and the whole state can smell it coming.","why":"From East & the Islands"},"sm-fk.sarhul-flowers":{"route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:scenes:4:text","title":"When the Sal Trees Bloom — the middle","body":"There is one custom inside Sarhul worth thinking about slowly. Until the festival is kept — until thanks are given — many families will not eat the new season's first fruits and flowers of the forest.","why":"A moment from When the Sal Trees Bloom"},"sl-fk.sarhul-flowers":{"route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:moral","title":"What When the Sal Trees Bloom says","body":"Begin the year with a thank-you, and everything eaten after it tastes like a gift.","why":"The story’s own lesson"},"sw-fk.sarhul-flowers-1":{"route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from When the Sal Trees Bloom","text":"वसंत","roman":"vasant","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["spring","a bathing","farewell, send-off"],"a":0,"after":"वसंत (vasant) means “spring”."},"why":"A word from When the Sal Trees Bloom"},"sw-fk.sarhul-flowers-2":{"route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from When the Sal Trees Bloom","text":"धन्यवाद","roman":"dhanyavaad","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["thanks","a promise","free kitchen"],"a":0,"after":"धन्यवाद (dhanyavaad) means “thanks”."},"why":"A word from When the Sal Trees Bloom"},"sp-fk.sarhul-flowers":{"route":"#/story/fk.sarhul-flowers","art":null,"cta":"Read the story","src":"story:fk.sarhul-flowers:place:0","title":"When the Sal Trees Bloom","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Jharkhand","Meghalaya","Tripura"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Jharkhand."},"why":"A map question"},"ep-ramayana-21":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:0:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"They camped in front of Lanka and Rama sent one more message before anything began — Angada, up over the wall, into the hall, with the same offer for the last time: give her back and keep everything. Ravana threw him out. So then there was a war, and it was not a short one.","why":"The Ramayana, night 21"},"eh-ramayana-21":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:hook","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights","body":"The war did not go well. And then Lakshmana went down.","why":"The Ramayana, night 21"},"em-ramayana-21":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:cards:6:text","title":"The Ramayana · The Long Nights — the middle","body":"Sushena, the army physician, got everyone out of the way and looked at him properly. \"He is not dead. He will be by sunrise. There is a herb — four of them, on a mountain called Dronagiri, north, in the high Himalaya. They glow at night.","why":"The Ramayana, night 21"},"ew-ramayana-21":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":21},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:20:wonder","title":"Something to wonder about","body":"Hanuman stood on a mountain in the dark and said out loud that he did not know which plant it was. What happens to a problem in the moment somebody admits they cannot solve it the way they meant to?","why":"The Ramayana, night 21"},"ep-ramayana-22":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:0:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"Mandodari caught him at the door of the hall on the last morning, in his armour, and stood in front of him. \"Even now,\" she said. \"Even this morning. Walk out with her and put her hand in his and it is over by lunchtime and you are still king of this city.\"","why":"The Ramayana, night 22"},"eh-ramayana-22":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:hook","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana","body":"He knew he was wrong. He had known for ten months. He came out anyway.","why":"The Ramayana, night 22"},"em-ramayana-22":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:cards:6:text","title":"The Ramayana · Ravana — the middle","body":"It ended in the evening, with one arrow, and this telling is going to leave it there, because what matters is not how it was done but what happened next.","why":"The Ramayana, night 22"},"ew-ramayana-22":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:wonder","title":"Something to wonder about","body":"Ravana stood in the doorway of his own hall knowing exactly what he ought to do, and said \"I cannot.\" What is it that a person cannot do, even when they can see what it is?","why":"The Ramayana, night 22"},"ewd-ramayana-22-0":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from Ravana","text":"रथ","roman":"rath","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["chariot","deity","ox, bullock"],"a":0,"after":"रथ (rath) means “chariot”."},"why":"A word from Ravana"},"ewd-ramayana-22-1":{"route":"#/epic/ramayana","act":{"a":"episode","id":"ramayana","n":22},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:ramayana:episodes:21:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from Ravana","text":"ज्ञान","roman":"gyaan","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["knowledge","equal","protection"],"a":0,"after":"ज्ञान (gyaan) means “knowledge”."},"why":"A word from Ravana"},"ep-mahabharata-14":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:0:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"The messenger went to the women's rooms and told her, and she did not scream and did not weep. She asked him a question and sent him back with it, and the question is the sharpest thing in the whole poem.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14"},"eh-mahabharata-14":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:hook","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question","body":"One question, asked in a room full of the greatest men alive. None of them could answer it.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14"},"em-mahabharata-14":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · Draupadi's Question — the middle","body":"Vidura said plainly that she had not been lost and was being wronged, and was ignored. And then one of the hundred stood up — Vikarna, Duryodhana's own brother — and said his brother was in the wrong and the woman had not been lawfully staked. The hundred are not one lump.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14"},"ew-mahabharata-14":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:wonder","title":"Something to wonder about","body":"Draupadi asked whether a man who had already lost himself could stake anybody else, and the wisest man in the room said he could not see the bottom of it. Nobody has answered her since. Is a question still worth asking if nobody can answer it?","why":"The Mahabharata, night 14"},"ewd-mahabharata-14-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":14},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:13:words_hi:1","title":"A Hindi word from Draupadi's Question","text":"जवाब","roman":"jawab","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["answer","coin","mirror"],"a":0,"after":"जवाब (jawab) means “answer”."},"why":"A word from Draupadi's Question"},"ep-mahabharata-15":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:0:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"They were on the road. The chariots were loaded. The kingdom had been handed back, the brothers were free, and for about an hour it looked as if the whole disaster was going to end in an apology and a long silence at family dinners.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15"},"eh-mahabharata-15":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:hook","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw","body":"The blind king gave it all back. Then he let his son ask for one more game.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15"},"em-mahabharata-15":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:cards:6:text","title":"The Mahabharata · One More Throw — the middle","body":"So a messenger caught the brothers on the road and brought them back for one game. One throw. And the stake was not a kingdom this time, it was a rule.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15"},"ew-mahabharata-15":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:wonder","title":"Something to wonder about","body":"Gandhari told her husband to send their own son away that morning, and he said he could not, because it was his son. Is there anything somebody you love could do that would stop you protecting them?","why":"The Mahabharata, night 15"},"ewd-mahabharata-15-0":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from One More Throw","text":"तेरह","roman":"terah","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["thirteen","coconut","mind"],"a":0,"after":"तेरह (terah) means “thirteen”."},"why":"A word from One More Throw"},"ewd-mahabharata-15-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":15},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:14:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from One More Throw","text":"साल","roman":"saal","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["year","conch","moonlight"],"a":0,"after":"साल (saal) means “year”."},"why":"A word from One More Throw"},"ep-mahabharata-16":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:0:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"The Vana Parva is the longest book in the whole poem, and almost nothing happens in it. Twelve years of walking, and camping, and listening to other people's stories, and having the same argument over and over again around a fire.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16"},"eh-mahabharata-16":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:hook","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest","body":"\"I am not asking you to be a good man. I am asking you to be angry for one hour.\"","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16"},"em-mahabharata-16":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:cards:6:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Longest Argument in the Forest — the middle","body":"Nobody wins that argument. It goes on for twelve years, and it is the most honest picture of a family under pressure anybody has ever written, and every part of it is somebody being right.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16"},"ew-mahabharata-16":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":16},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:15:wonder","title":"Something to wonder about","body":"Draupadi asked her husband to be angry for one hour and he would not, and they had that argument for twelve years and never settled it. Can two people both be right and still not be able to live with each other's answer?","why":"The Mahabharata, night 16"},"ep-mahabharata-17":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:0:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"Arjuna went north by himself, up past the snow line, to ask for weapons they were going to need. He stood on one leg for a long time and ate almost nothing and did not speak, and the mountain took its time about noticing.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17"},"eh-mahabharata-17":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:hook","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain","body":"Two arrows hit the same boar at the same moment. Both archers claimed it.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17"},"em-mahabharata-17":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:cards:6:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Hunter on the Mountain — the middle","body":"So he did the only thing he had left. He scraped a little heap of earth together into a rough shape, the way you would make a figure at home, and he picked the flowers that grow up there and put them on its head, and bowed.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17"},"ew-mahabharata-17":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":17},"art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:16:wonder","title":"Something to wonder about","body":"Shiva told Arjuna that nobody climbs that mountain to be told they are wonderful. What is a person actually looking for when they go looking for a teacher?","why":"The Mahabharata, night 17"},"ih-souths":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:hook","title":"Ships, Bronze and Stone · about 300 BCE–1600 CE","body":"South Indian kings sent fleets across the Bay of Bengal — and their sculptors cast bronzes nobody has bettered."},"ik-souths":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:kid","title":"Ships, Bronze and Stone","body":"The Cholas, Pallavas, Cheras, Pandyas and later Vijayanagara built temples you can still walk into. Chola bronzes of Nataraja are among the finest metal sculptures made anywhere. Tamil Sangam poetry is older than most European literature."},"ib-souths":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:big","title":"Ships, Bronze and Stone — more","body":"Chola influence reached Southeast Asia through trade and naval expeditions, which is part of why you find Ramayana stories in Java and Thailand today."},"iw-souths":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:wonder","title":"Nobody knows yet","body":"Hampi was one of the largest cities in the world in the 1500s. You can still stand in its market street."},"io-souths-0":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:objects:0","title":"Found from Ships, Bronze and Stone","body":"A Chola Nataraja bronze"},"io-souths-1":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:objects:1","title":"Found from Ships, Bronze and Stone","body":"The stone chariot at Hampi"},"io-souths-2":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:objects:2","title":"Found from Ships, Bronze and Stone","body":"Shore temple at Mamallapuram"},"it-souths-m0":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:moments:0:what","title":"about 300 BCE–200 CE","body":"The Sangam age: Tamil poets compose about love, war, rain and the sea — poetry people still read today."},"it-souths-m1":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:moments:1:what","title":"the early centuries CE","body":"Roman ships ride the monsoon winds to Kerala's ports for pepper — Roman coins still turn up in south Indian fields."},"it-souths-m2":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:moments:2:what","title":"the 600s CE","body":"The Pallavas carve whole temples out of living rock at Mamallapuram, right down to the shore."},"it-souths-m3":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:moments:3:what","title":"about 850 CE onwards","body":"The Cholas rise in the Kaveri delta — their story gets its own stretch of this river."},"it-souths-m4":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:moments:4:what","title":"1336 onwards","body":"Vijayanagara is founded and grows into one of the largest cities on Earth — travellers from Persia and Portugal write home amazed."},"it-souths-m5":{"route":"#/era/souths","cta":"Sail to Ships, Bronze and Stone","art":null,"sources":["Chola inscriptions at Thanjavur","Sangam corpus","Accounts of visitors to Vijayanagara, including Domingo Paes","Roman coin hoards found in Tamil Nadu and Kerala","ASI, the Mamallapuram monuments"],"why":"Ships, Bronze and Stone, about 300 BCE–1600 CE","src":"era:souths:moments:5:what","title":"1565","body":"After the battle of Talikota the great city is abandoned. Hampi's ruins are what remains."}});
